  array['English','Kannada']::text[], array['academy','blended']::text[], 'Hygiene Certificate', 12, true, true, true,
  'A 6-day, 36-hour programme teaching the exact cleaning routines, chemical safety and waste handling used in every VTIARA branch.', 'Housekeeping staff keep treatment rooms safe and clean; one missed step can spread infection. The 6 days (16 hours of Common Core plus 20 hours of housekeeping) are taught mostly through pictures and demonstrations, in Kannada, Hindi or the local language, with English terms for chemicals and equipment. You may never touch used sharps, handle lasers or other devices, move patient records, or enter a procedure room during a procedure without a nurse or doctor present.', 'Housekeeping and support staff, new hires and existing staff.', array['8th to 10th pass; can read labels and simple instructions','Medical fitness check','Hepatitis B vaccination recommended before joining']::text[], array['Clean and reset treatment rooms, waiting areas, washrooms and corridors','Mix and use approved disinfectants as taught','Segregate and move biomedical waste to the storage area','Handle linen and towels safely','Clean small spills using the spill kit']::text[], array['100% attendance','80% or higher on the knowledge test','All practical checklist items passed','Yearly refresher']::text[], '{"attendance_pct":100,"knowledge_pct":80,"practical":"Practical checklist passed","trainer_signoff":true}'::jsonb) on conflict (id) do nothing;
- Clean first, then disinfect: dirt blocks disinfectants
- Colour-coded cloths and mops: red for toilets and washroom floors, yellow for washbasins and taps, blue for treatment rooms and reception, green for pantry', 1) on conflict (id) do nothing;
- Read the label every time; wear gloves and, where the label says so, eye protection
- Never mix chemicals; bleach mixed with acids or ammonia products gives toxic gas
- Keep every bottle labelled; never pour chemicals into drink bottles
- Practice: identify 10 products from labels; match product to area', 2) on conflict (id) do nothing;
- Dilution and contact time come from the product label; typical chlorine strengths 0.1% (1,000 ppm) for general surfaces and 1% (10,000 ppm) for blood or body-fluid spills; confirm with the branch policy
- Make fresh solution each day and label it with date and time', 1) on conflict (id) do nothing;
- Clean to dirty, top to bottom, far to near; use two-bucket mopping
- High-touch surfaces first: door handles, switches, bed rails, trolley handles, chair arms, taps', 2) on conflict (id) do nothing;
- Treatment room turnover between patients, end-of-day deep clean, washroom routine
- Spill response (SOP-HK-04)
- Practice: dilution exercise with measuring cups; timed cleaning of a mock treatment room (target 10 minutes)', 3) on conflict (id) do nothing;
- Yellow: soiled dressings, cotton, gauze, contaminated items, expired medicines; red: contaminated plastics such as tubing, syringes without needles, gloves
- White translucent puncture-proof container: needles and blades; blue box: glass vials and ampoules; black bag: general waste
- Confirm current Biomedical Waste Management Rules, 2016 and your waste vendor''s instructions', 1) on conflict (id) do nothing;
- Never mix categories; fill bags only up to three-quarters; never press down a bag; never put hands inside; label with date and branch
- Keep a waste register; store in the locked waste room; hand over only to the authorised vendor', 2) on conflict (id) do nothing;
- Linen: wear gloves, do not shake, carry in a closed bag, keep soiled linen apart
- Laser room: enter only when the nurse says the device is off; never touch a device or move cables
- Never touch a loose needle or blade: stay away and call the nurse
- Practice: sort 30 items into bins; visit the waste room and read the register', 3) on conflict (id) do nothing;

